Call of Duty 2: Big Red One - native loader for PC, prototype @PROJECT_VERSION@
===================================================================

A native loader for @COD_BRO_README_SYSTEM@: it runs the original Xbox game's code directly
on your PC's CPU (the Xbox is an x86 machine too), with the Xbox's system
software (kernel, Direct3D, DirectSound, controllers) replaced by our own code.
The CPU is not emulated and nothing is recompiled. It needs your own copy of the
game; no game code or data is included.

This is a prototype and the proof of concept for a full native build: the
whole single-player campaign is playable.
Multiplayer works over System Link (your local network); Xbox Live is not
available.


Setup
-----

Copy your game dump into the assets folder.

Then start cod_bro_launcher. It shows whether the game files are found, lets
you use another folder instead, sets the options below, and starts the game.
(cod_bro_loader starts the game directly with the saved settings.)

On Linux, run ./install-linux-icon.sh once to put the supplied icon and a
"Call of Duty 2: Big Red One" entry in your desktop's applications menu. On
Windows the icon is already embedded in cod_bro_launcher.exe and
cod_bro_loader.exe.


Requirements
------------

@COD_BRO_README_REQUIREMENTS@


If the launcher window will not open
-----------------------------------

On some Wayland compositors SDL3 has no software framebuffer and no usable EGL,
so the launcher's 2D renderer cannot be created ("Couldn't find matching render
driver"). The launcher prefers X11 when DISPLAY is available (X11 works on a
native X11 session and through XWayland), tries every available render driver,
and retries with the X11 backend if all fail. You can force one yourself:

  SDL_VIDEO_DRIVER=x11 ./cod_bro_launcher        X11 / XWayland
  COD_BRO_VIDEO_DRIVER=x11 ./cod_bro_launcher   the same, our own override
  SDL_VIDEO_DRIVER=wayland ./cod_bro_launcher   native Wayland
  SDL_RENDER_DRIVER=software ./cod_bro_launcher force the 2D software renderer


Controls
--------

Gamepads use their Xbox layout. Keyboard and mouse:

  WASD           move                 mouse          look
  left mouse     fire                 right mouse    aim down sights
  Space          jump                 C / Ctrl       crouch, prone
  R              reload               F              use
  G              grenade              V / middle     melee
  left Shift     sprint               Q / E          lean
  wheel / 1      next weapon          Tab (hold)     objectives / scores
  Escape         pause                F10            free the mouse
  F4             Modding window (PNG textures, WAV sounds, hands/weapons/anims)

Menus: point with the mouse and click (right click goes back, the wheel
moves), or arrow keys, Return selects, Backspace goes back. In a level, click
the window to capture the mouse; Alt+Tab or F10 frees it.


Modding (F4)
------------

Press F4 in a level to open the Modding window. It has three tabs:

  Textures:   Extract writes every DDS picture the game has loaded as PNG into
              <user data>/mods/textures/. Replace reads those PNGs back and
              swaps them into the running game immediately (any size is resized).
  Audio:      Extract writes the game's wave banks and loose sounds as 16-bit
              PCM WAV into <user data>/mods/audio/ with audio.csv (hash, kind,
              channels, rate, exact byte length). Replace converts edited WAVs
              back to the original encoding and writes patched copies into
              <user data>/mods/overrides/. Restart or reload the level to hear
              them; delete a file there to restore the original sound.
  Animations: Extract exports ALL player view-hands, EVERY weapon model and
              EVERY animation (.xbmesh/.xbcell/.xbmorph/.xmb/.xbanim/.xbsanim/
              .xbskel) from the .cod packs into <user data>/mods/animations/:
                viewhands/         first-person hands models
                weapons/<weapon>/  one folder per weapon (models + animations)
                animations/        every other NAL clip
                models/ skeletons/ everything else
              animations.csv records each asset's pack, byte offset, exact size,
              content hash and group. Replace patches edited SAME-LENGTH files
              into copies of the game files in <user data>/mods/overrides/;
              restart or reload the level to see them. These are the game's
              original Xbox formats, not glTF/FBX - no data is converted.

All three tabs show extraction and replacement progress while they work.
Original files in assets/ are never changed. <user data> is
~/.local/share/cod_bro on Linux (or $XDG_DATA_HOME/cod_bro) and
%APPDATA%\cod_bro on Windows.


Discord Rich Presence
---------------------

Discord RPC is on by default and can be switched off in the launcher's General
tab, with --discord-rpc off, or with COD_BRO_DISCORD_RPC=0. It uses Discord's
local IPC only: no account token or network access. The launcher shows "In the
launcher"; in game it shows "Playing Call of Duty 2: Big Red One" with only the
map name (no mission number), or "Multiplayer - System Link" when no map is
loaded. App ID: 1567972853180010607.
Discord must be running; otherwise the game just silently skips it.


Multiplayer
-----------

Start it with the launcher's Multiplayer button, or from the game's own
Multiplayer menu item. Play System Link: one player picks Create Match, the
others Find Match. It works between PCs on the same local network and between
two copies of the game on one PC. Every PC needs the same System Link port
(launcher; default UDP 3074-3077), and a firewall has to let those ports
through on the local network.


Options
-------

The launcher saves its settings in @COD_BRO_README_SETTINGS@, next to
the saves. Every setting is also a command-line option of cod_bro_loader, which
then wins over the file; @COD_BRO_README_HELP@ lists them.


Reporting a problem
-------------------

Every start writes log.txt next to settings.ini; the "Open log folder" button
on the launcher's Help tab opens that folder, and "Open save folder" the
saves. Attach log.txt to your report (copy it first: the next start replaces
it), plus the save folder if the problem depends on your progress.

For a low frame rate, tick "Measure performance" on the Help tab before pressing
Play and play to where it gets slow: the log then also says where the time went.


